# Target C — audited §153 exclusion of the corrected Type-II `q-k` endpoint

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only route module; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module normalizes the theorem and independent hostile audit previously carried by PR #401.
It proves that the corrected Type-II scalar lower endpoint

\[
t_*:=\frac25\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right),
\qquad
\Delta_2:=\frac\pi{2\sqrt3},
\tag{153.1}
\]

cannot be attained by an unbounded Type-II C-II survivor sequence:

\[
\boxed{
\frac{q-k}{D}\not\longrightarrow
\frac25\left(\pi+\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}\right)
=1.6375621841815\ldots .
}
\tag{153.2}
\]

The proof uses only the canonical full-core Type-II ledgers through the audit.46 authority cutoff, together with elementary graph stability and convex geometry. It does not use a response theorem, a KKT coefficient floor, post-126 proof-only structure, or any extension of the Section-57 affine kernel to contact-isolated source points.

The mechanism is rigid: endpoint saturation forces the strict-rhombus train graph to be asymptotically Mantel-extremal and forces the occurrence-counted outer contact walk to be asymptotically perimeter-minimizing. The first condition collapses almost all train directions to three projective axes; the second is impossible for a near-disk closed walk supported asymptotically on only those three axes.

---

## 153.1 Exact nonnegative slack decomposition

Work in the canonical full-core Type-II branch. Let

- `q` be the number of loaded force-hull vertices;
- `k=d_H+s_Z`;
- `b` be the occurrence-counted outer contact-face walk length;
- `Q` be the bounded nonquadrilateral face degree excess;
- `f` be the number of bounded nonquadrilateral contact faces;
- `R_4` be the number of strict unit-rhombus bounded faces;
- `B_4` be the physical boundary-edge occurrence count of the full strict-rhombus complex;
- `F` be the force hull; and
- `p=per(F)`.

Canonical Section 63 gives

\[
b+Q=2(q-k).
\tag{153.3}
\]

Canonical Section 85 gives

\[
q-k+2f\ge2\sqrt{R_4}.
\tag{153.4}
\]

Also

\[
f\le Q,
\qquad
b\ge p.
\tag{153.5}
\]

Put

\[
t:=q-k,
\qquad
r:=\sqrt{R_4}.
\]

Then the identity

\[
\boxed{
5t-2p-2r
=(t+2f-2r)+2(Q-f)+2(b-p)
}
\tag{153.6}
\]

is exact. Every term on the right is nonnegative. Therefore

\[
\boxed{5(q-k)\ge2\operatorname{per}(F)+2\sqrt{R_4}.}
\tag{153.7}
\]

Canonical Type II gives

\[
\frac{p}{D}\to\pi,
\qquad
\frac{R_4}{D^2}\to\Delta_2.
\tag{153.8}
\]

Thus (153.7) recovers the corrected endpoint floor

\[
\liminf\frac{q-k}{D}\ge t_*.
\tag{153.9}
\]

The rejected historical value `1.9046256...` is not used.

---

## 153.2 Endpoint saturation kills every scalar slack

Assume for contradiction that along an unbounded Type-II survivor sequence

\[
\frac{q-k}{D}\to t_*.
\tag{153.10}
\]

By (153.6), (153.8), and the definition of `t_*`, the sum of three nonnegative terms is `o(D)`. Hence each is `o(D)`:

\[
\boxed{
A_D:=t+2f-2r=o(D),
\qquad
Q-f=o(D),
\qquad
b-p=o(D).
}
\tag{153.11}
\]

In particular the outer contact walk is asymptotically geodesic relative to the force-hull perimeter.

Canonical Section 85 has the boundary-incidence estimate

\[
B_4\le b+4f+Q.
\tag{153.12}
\]

Using (153.3),

\[
b+4f+Q=2t+4f=4r+2A_D.
\tag{153.13}
\]

The strict-rhombus train theorem gives

\[
B_4\ge4r.
\tag{153.14}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{B_4=4r+o(D).}
\tag{153.15}
\]

Let `T` be the number of stopped strict-rhombus train tracks. Each train has exactly two physical boundary ends, so

\[
T=B_4/2=2r+o(D).
\tag{153.16}
\]

Every strict rhombus is exactly one crossing of two trains. The canonical train-crossing graph is simple and triangle-free, hence

\[
R_4\le T^2/4.
\]

Equations (153.15)–(153.16) give

\[
\boxed{
\frac{T^2}{4}-R_4=o(D^2).
}
\tag{153.17}
\]

Thus the train-crossing graph is asymptotically Mantel-extremal.

---

## 153.3 Elementary near-Mantel stability

### Lemma 153.1

Let `G_D` be triangle-free graphs with `N_D=Theta(D)` vertices and

\[
e(G_D)=N_D^2/4-o(D^2).
\]

Then there is a partition

\[
V(G_D)=X_D\sqcup Y_D
\]

such that

\[
|X_D|=N_D/2+o(D),
\qquad
|Y_D|=N_D/2+o(D),
\tag{153.18}
\]

`X_D` is independent, the number of edges inside `Y_D` is `o(D^2)`, and only `o(D^2)` pairs in `X_D\times Y_D` are missing.

### Proof

Choose a maximum-degree vertex `v`, put `X=N(v)`, `d=|X|`, and `Y=V\setminus X`. Triangle-freeness makes `X` independent. If `e_Y` is the number of edges inside `Y` and `e_{XY}` the cross-edge count, maximality of `d` gives

\[
e_{XY}+2e_Y
=\sum_{y\in Y}\deg y
\le d|Y|=d(N-d).
\]

Since `e=e_{XY}+e_Y`,

\[
e+e_Y\le d(N-d)\le N^2/4.
\]

The near-extremal hypothesis yields `e_Y=o(D^2)` and

\[
d(N-d)=N^2/4-o(D^2),
\]

so `d=N/2+o(D)`. Finally

\[
d(N-d)-e_{XY}
=[d(N-d)-e]+e_Y=o(D^2).
\]

This proves the lemma. \(\square\)

Apply Lemma 153.1 to the train-crossing graph using (153.17).

---

## 153.4 The angular-defect budget collapses train directions to three axes

Let

\[
\phi_\tau\in\mathbb R/\pi\mathbb Z
\]

be the projective direction of train `tau`. If two trains cross in a strict rhombus, their acute projective separation is

\[
\operatorname{dist}(\phi_\tau,\phi_\sigma)
=\frac\pi3+\delta_{\tau\sigma},
\qquad
0<\delta_{\tau\sigma}\le\frac\pi6.
\tag{153.19}
\]

Canonical Section 68 supplies the total angular-defect budget

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{\tau\sigma\in E(G)}\delta_{\tau\sigma}=O(D).
}
\tag{153.20}
\]

Take

\[
\eta_D=D^{-1/3}.
\]

From Lemma 153.1, the missing `X`–`Y` pairs are `o(D^2)`. A two-threshold averaging argument gives an anchor `x_0\in X` having simultaneously

- only `o(D)` nonneighbors in `Y`; and
- total incident angular defect `O(1)`.

For the first property, if the missing-pair count is `\mu_DD^2` with `\mu_D\to0`, all but `O(\sqrt{\mu_D}D)=o(D)` vertices of `X` miss only `O(\sqrt{\mu_D}D)=o(D)` partners. For the second, (153.20) leaves a fixed positive fraction of `X` with bounded incident defect. The two good sets intersect for large `D`.

Therefore all but `o(D)` members of `Y` joined to `x_0` have defect at most `eta_D`, so their directions lie in shrinking neighborhoods of

\[
\phi_{x_0}+\pi/3,
\qquad
\phi_{x_0}-\pi/3.
\tag{153.21}
\]

One of these two clusters contains `Omega(D)` members; call it `Y_*`, centered at `beta_D`.

A pair in `X\times Y_*` is bad if it is missing or if its crossing defect exceeds `eta_D`. Missing pairs contribute `o(D^2)`, while (153.20) gives at most

\[
O(D/\eta_D)=O(D^{4/3})=o(D^2)
\]

large-defect crossings. Hence all but `o(D)` members of `X` have a good partner in `Y_*`, which puts their directions within `o(1)` of one of the two projective directions at distance `pi/3` from `beta_D`.

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\text{all but }o(D)\text{ trains have projective direction }o(1)
\text{ from three axes separated by }\pi/3.
}
\tag{153.22}
\]

No phase labels or external graph-stability theorem are used.

---

## 153.5 Boundary saturation transfers the three-axis collapse to the outer walk

Define

\[
U_D:=b+4f+Q-B_4\ge0.
\tag{153.23}
\]

Equations (153.13) and (153.14) imply

\[
0\le U_D\le2A_D=o(D).
\tag{153.24}
\]

The Section-85 boundary classification is occurrence-counted: `b+4f+Q` is exactly the number of eligible face-side occurrences on the old outer face and bounded nonquadrilateral faces, while `B_4` is exactly the number of those occurrences whose opposite side is a strict rhombus. Thus `U_D` is the exact unused occurrence count, not merely a coarse numerical difference.

Hence all but `o(D)` outer-walk edge occurrences border a strict rhombus. Every such occurrence is a terminal boundary edge of one stopped train. Since each train has exactly two physical boundary ends, the `o(D)` direction-outlier trains from (153.22) account for only `o(D)` outer occurrences. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\text{all but }o(D)\text{ unit-edge occurrences of the outer contact walk lie within }o(1)
\text{ of the same three projective axes.}
}
\tag{153.25}
\]

Bridge and articulation repetitions are harmless because `b` and the boundary classification retain occurrence multiplicity.

---

## 153.6 Projection variation forbids a three-axis near-geodesic walk around a disk

Write the occurrence-counted closed outer walk as

\[
p_0,p_1,\ldots,p_b=p_0,
\qquad
|p_{j+1}-p_j|=1.
\]

All loaded force-hull vertices occur on this walk in cyclic order, and all walk vertices lie in the positive core inside `F`, so

\[
\operatorname{conv}\{p_0,\ldots,p_{b-1}\}=F.
\tag{153.26}
\]

For

\[
n_\theta=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)
\]

define

\[
V_D(\theta)
:=\sum_{j=0}^{b-1}
\left|(p_{j+1}-p_j)\cdot n_\theta\right|.
\tag{153.27}
\]

Let `w_F(theta)` be the width of `F` in direction `n_theta`. A closed real-valued walk that visits both the minimum and maximum support values has total variation at least twice their separation, even with repeated vertices. Thus

\[
\boxed{V_D(\theta)\ge2w_F(\theta).}
\tag{153.28}
\]

Each unit edge satisfies

\[
\int_0^\pi|\cos(\theta-\phi)|\,d\theta=2,
\]

so

\[
\int_0^\pi V_D(\theta)\,d\theta=2b.
\tag{153.29}
\]

Cauchy's perimeter formula gives

\[
\int_0^\pi w_F(\theta)\,d\theta=p.
\tag{153.30}
\]

Therefore

\[
\int_0^\pi\left(V_D(\theta)-2w_F(\theta)\right)d\theta
=2(b-p)=o(D).
\tag{153.31}
\]

The integrand is nonnegative. Canonical Type-II near-disk convergence gives uniformly

\[
\frac{w_F(\theta)}D\to1.
\tag{153.32}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{V_D/D\to2\quad\text{in }L^1([0,\pi]).}
\tag{153.33}
\]

By (153.25), after subsequence extraction the three axis centers converge to

\[
\alpha,
\quad
\alpha+\pi/3,
\quad
\alpha+2\pi/3,
\]

and their edge-count ratios converge to `c_0,c_1,c_2>=0`. Absolute cosine is 1-Lipschitz; the exceptional-edge count is `o(D)`; and `b=O(D)`. Therefore the same normalized projection variation converges **uniformly** to

\[
\boxed{
g(\theta)=\sum_{i=0}^2c_i\left|\cos\left(\theta-\alpha-i\pi/3\right)\right|.}
\tag{153.34}
\]

The uniform limit (153.34) and the `L^1` limit (153.33) force `g=2` almost everywhere, hence everywhere by continuity.

This is impossible. Away from the finitely many cosine-zero cusp angles, all signs are fixed, so on every remaining open interval

\[
g(\theta)=A\cos\theta+B\sin\theta.
\tag{153.35}
\]

Such a function cannot equal the positive constant `2` on an open interval: differentiating forces `A=B=0`, after which `g=0` there. Contradiction.

This proves (153.2).

---

## 153.7 Scope of the gap conclusion

The theorem proves **endpoint unattainability** for the Type-II asymptotic class. A qualitative separation from `t_*` follows in the sequence/diagonal sense: a family of Type-II survivor sequences whose limiting `(q-k)/D` values decreased to `t_*` could be diagonalized into the forbidden endpoint behavior while preserving the canonical Type-II vanishing normalized container parameters.

What is **not** proved is a finite explicit inequality of the form

\[
q-k\ge(t_*+\varepsilon_0)D-C
\]

for every individual Type-II configuration, nor is any numerical `epsilon_0` certified.

---

## 153.8 Frontier effect

The corrected scalar Type-II loaded-hull endpoint is structurally impossible. Any surviving Type-II sequence must remain separated from it in the asymptotic sense or pay additional topology/outer-walk/face currency.

This does not close C-II:

- the macroscopic inactive-container branch remains open;
- Type-II sequences away from this endpoint remain open;
- no exact unit equilateral triangle is forced; and
- no same-cardinality strict sub-`D` competitor is constructed.

**Disposition:** audited-working proof-only theorem. C-II and Level I remain open.
